  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSurface high pressure will help keep pleasant conditions around \nthrough mid-week. The exception will be on Tuesday as was warm front \nlifts north of the region. The short-range guidance such as the \nNAMNest and other HREF members continue to show scattered showers \nfrom early Tuesday morning through the afternoon hours. The longer-\nrange models either keep the region dry or have showers across the \nfar northern areas. With this much uncertainty, PoPs were increased \nfrom the NBM solution with help from Conshort and manual \nadjustments. As a result, there are slight chance PoPs across \nCentral NY, mainly north of the Southern Tier. Model soundings show \na pocket of dry air near the surface, so precipitation is expected \nto be light.\n\nOtherwise, an upper ridge builds into the region. Southwesterly flow \nwill advect in warmer air, allowing temperatures to trend warmer \nthrough Wednesday. Under sunny skies, 70s return for daytime highs \nfor the majority of the region on Wednesday. Nightly temperatures \nwill be mild as well with lows in the 40s and 50s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe latter half of the week will become more active as multiple \ndisturbances bring rain to the region. A low pressure system over \nthe Midwest will propagate into the Great Lakes region. As it does, \nthis will push a front through with rain moving in early Thursday \nmorning. Unlike previous model runs, the front moves through quickly \nas the coastal low that was once present now develops further out at \nsea. With this system, PWATs will be 1.2 to 1.5 inches, which is 2-3 \nstandard deviations above normal for this time of year. Instability \nwill be limited but with higher shear present, a few thunderstorms \nmay be possible. Despite this, hydro concerns are minimal as this \nfront is quick to move through following an extended dry period. If \nthere were to be any ponding, it will likely be due to leaf litter \nclogging up drains. \n\nA slot of dry air fills in behind the front. However, an upper level \ntrough is trailing this system and will support additional showers \nFriday and Saturday as wraparound moisture nudges in from the north. \nWhile there is some uncertainty on how long the showers will last, \nsurface high pressure returns for the second half of the weekend.",
